A||||11||speed racer||glowing rotors||||1||
Z||000000||speed racer||12-04-2001||10:23 PM||||I was driving my car today and I pulled over to try to get my head light up and I looked down and my rear drivers side rotor was glowing it was so hot. Then the breaks went out almost all together I know the break was dragging. I was going to rebuild rear the calipers. Do you think this will fix my problem?<BR>Kyle<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||West Coast Fiero||12-04-2001||10:58 PM||||first I would see that the e-brake  cable isnt over-adjusted. On the caliper levers that operate the brake pads during ebrake operation you want to ensure that they have a spacing of 1/16" ( locator on caliper, this is where the lever makes contact with the caliper in its rotation ) from the cast in stop on the caliper. The adjustment nut on the e-brake cable requires a 16mm end wrench<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000002||speed racer||12-04-2001||11:39 PM||||Would that have any thing to do with the rest of the breaks going out though.<BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||12-04-2001||11:45 PM||||I would bet that because you drove it that way the rotors also need to be replaced. If you haven't worn them below minimum thickness, chances are they're warped from all the heat. If all that heat transmitted to the rear wheel bearings (check for grease leak, they're supposed to be sealed) they be toast shortly.||||reg||1
Z||000004||scarydok||12-05-2001||12:20 AM||||I can tell you from experience that it was more than likely your brake lines.  It had me going nuts when it happened to me (driving a 88 Celebrity, 2.8 V-6).  I thought it was that the caliper was locked up, but it was the brake line.  I don't know all the tech info on it, but it has to do with the pressure in the line.  If your line is clogged or just bad, then your caliper will lock up.||||reg||1
Z||000005||honegod||12-12-2001||05:50 AM||||the normal swelling of the inside of the flex hoses that takes place after about 10 to 15 years eventually blocks off fluid flow to and from the caliper, since the brake pedal generates a lot more pressure than the caliper piston the pressure in the caliper builds up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tigger||12-12-2001||07:10 AM||||First check the E-brake adjustment.  Next, I'd recommend replacing the calipers with rebuilds, and since you will need to bleed the system, replace the flex hoses (soft lines).  You could try just replacing the soft lines, bleed and see if that solves it.  If the calipers are real old it's probably that time.  The rotor maybe toast, hard to say.  I had a front caliper get hot on me once and it turned out to be the slides on the calipers were bunged up, that could also be a possibility.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||TTiger||12-12-2001||01:12 PM||||I would agree with everyone else.  E brake first, rebuild the calipers (or replace) second.  Replace the rotors right away, too. Don't take everything apart twice.  Same thing happened to all 4 of my disk brakes on my Plymouth Laser a week after I bought it. What a piece of SH!@ that car was.<P>My .02  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Tony ||||reg||14
Z||000008||speed racer||12-12-2001||04:54 PM||||Well the break hose was clogged I got a new one but don't have it installed yet.<BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000009||honegod||12-12-2001||07:58 PM||||you should REALLY replace the other wheels hoses too, they will be doing the same exact thing soon, it is an inherent flaw in the material they are made from.||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||12-13-2001||12:18 AM||||GET EARLS TEFLON LINES!!!<P>Yes, I'm yelling it.  $100 for a set of 4, never worry about this BS again.<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
